A||||9||Richard Parnell||Status of radiator fan problem||||1||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||11-03-2001||07:33 PM||||I was wondering if someone might be able to help me.  I'm having an odd problem with my Fiero.  Recently I noticed that the fan for my heater/vent does not work.  I came to discover that the fan for my radiator also does not work. (After the engine overheated)  After fooling around and testing various things such as the radiator fan motor, fuses, etc. I discovered that the two fuses 1. for radiator fan 2. for heater/vent/AC fan have no power coming to them.  All the other fuses have 12 volts.  After looking at the diagrams I see that many things are powered from fusable link A. So fusable link A is ok.  The only place I can see where there may be the problem is the ignition switch.  This is shown in the Haynes maual page 271 showing the power distribution.  Do you think this is the correct diagnosis?  I am at a loss otherwise in how to fix this.  Someone told me to jumper a wire from the accessory fuse to the fan fuse but I am afraid this may cause other problems.  Any help would be appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leadfoot||11-03-2001||09:07 PM||||Did you jumper the temp switch connector located on the intake manifold (v6) to see if the rad fan turns on.  Might be that the temp switch is bad.  As for the heater fan does it work at all?  If it works in high but not low or med then the resistor block could be bad.  Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||11-05-2001||11:48 AM||||The fan is a single speed.  It's on all the time when the ignition switch is on. I'm pretty sure the problem is in the ignition switch in the steering.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||11-05-2001||12:04 PM||||Sounds like a previous owner by-passed the fuse box and wired directly into the ignition. You might have to get into your steering column if you want to fix the problem. Pull down your fuse box (squeeze both sides in, and the fuse box folds down) and see if there are any wires going to where the fuse is. If not, they must have wired them into the column, with no fuse. That might be why your radiator fan motor fried. <P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">              <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||11-06-2001||03:17 AM||||The motor is not fried.  there is no powerto the fuse for the motor.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Leadfoot||11-06-2001||08:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Richard Parnell:<BR><B>The fan is a single speed.  It's on all the time when the ignition switch is on. I'm pretty sure the problem is in the ignition switch in the steering.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Rich, the heater fan should be multi-speed.  I think you confused it with the rad fan which is single speed for 6 and dual for 4.  So are you saying your rad fan turns on and stays on when you turn the ignition to on?  If so Sqouch is right someone jerry rigged it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indyrestoguy_JR||11-06-2001||09:43 AM||||Do you have power windows? Do they work?<BR>If not, check the orange wire from the start switch mounted low on the steering column for +12 volts. This wire supplies the fuse box power to both motors.<BR>Is the high speed fan (heater/AC) working?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Richard Parnell||11-06-2001||01:14 PM||||The heater fan is a multi speed.  The radiator fan is a single speed.  The radiator fan is on as soon as you turn on the ignition switch.  The radiator fan is fused.  There is no power to the fuse for the radiator, heater fan, nor the unused power window fuse.<BR> My car does not have a fan in the engine compartment.  I'm pretty sure the problem is in the apparatus that the key activates when turned.  I'm unfamiliar with that device and need to know if that unit can be worked on or should be replaced.||||reg||1
Z||000008||sqoach||11-06-2001||01:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Richard Parnell:<BR><B>The motor is not fried.  there is no powerto the fuse for the motor.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My bad. Thought I saw where you said it was.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Leadfoot||11-06-2001||09:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Richard Parnell:<BR><B>My car does not have a fan in the engine compartment.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I thought the same with my car until I looked at the wiring schematics in the haynes manual.  Turns out that there is a blower motor.  It's located under the carpet on the right side of the trunk. <P>||||reg||1
